import{_ as n,P as a,d as i,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.28 Technology, Infrastructure, Platforms, and Artificial Intelligence","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.28 Technology, Infrastructure, Platforms, and Artificial Intelligence","xkChars":0,"xkWords":621,"xkLessons":{"D.28":{"title":"D.28 Technology, Infrastructure, Platforms, and Artificial Intelligence","lead":"Technical outcomes arise from specific combinations of physical properties, configuration, institutions, and maintenance. Nominal approval, tool requests, and changes in real-world states cannot be conflated.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5420–5435, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-28#section-d-28. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘It is not “half technology and half society.”’ Bandwidth, materials, and interfaces impose independent constraints; default configurations and commercial arrangements can alter reach. A specific outcome requires describing the combination of physical properties, protocols, deployment, institutions, maintenance, and use, rather than replacing mechanisms with a slogan about proportions. [T-main]","Conformity to a written standard, interoperable implementation, shared business semantics, reliability under stress, and human usability are different layers. A message passing through does not prove both sides understand the same business object; working normally does not prove reliability under stress. Maintenance also needs verifiable spare parts, skills, budgets, powers, and continuing labor; it does not arise naturally once equipment is installed. [T-main]","When multiple devices share an upstream power supply or the same defective software, numbers do not guarantee independent redundancy. A comparison of necessity must specify whether it holds constant a product name, binary, configuration, or a complete deployment including training, powers, and maintenance. After changing configuration or training, one cannot say only an abstract institution changed. Substitutes must preserve the task, resources, deadline, and necessary protections. [T-main]","Platform matching and time flexibility can produce real benefits while pricing, eligibility, future opportunities, and appeals remain centrally controlled. The presence of an algorithm cannot directly determine every aspect of workers' legal status; specific rules require separate checking. Benefits do not cancel questions of power, and centralized control does not erase benefits. [T-main]","Judge attribution of AI-generated content, existence of a bibliographic item, metadata, support in the original text, facts about the world, and permission for the intended use separately. Retrieval helps locate material without guaranteeing truth. Internal consistency can reveal contradictions but can also stably repeat the same error. A limited reanalysis or synthetic recalculation may correct one quantity without proving corresponding improvement for actual users. [T-main]","Recommendation, tool request, program execution, external state change, and consequence form five stages. Oversight requires evidence, capacity, time before action, and a manageable workload; a refusal signal must actually reach the executor. The source's example of a user clicking reject after the backend has become irreversible shows why nominal approval is insufficient, not that every backend action is necessarily irreversible. [T-main]","Historical reference: paragraphs 15–22 of the SEC's October 16, 2013 settled administrative order concerning Knight describe a 2012 deployment in which one of eight servers was not updated, there was no review by a second person, and 97 emails were not designed as alerts. Identical product or code names do not guarantee identical deployed states. Sending information also does not mean people understand it and act in time. [P-SEC]","This account follows the findings recorded in the order and preserves its status as a settlement in which the respondent neither admitted nor denied the findings, with exceptions such as jurisdiction. It is not rewritten as a criminal conclusion. The case supports only the deployment-review and alert-to-action chain; it cannot alone explain all losses or establish that any algorithm is inherently unsafe. [P-SEC]","Privacy requires separate checks of collection, inference, reuse, retention, and downstream deletion. Sending an instruction does not prove completion. Actual possession, permitted use, accounting recognition, transaction eligibility, and cross-border eligibility differ. The Ministry of Finance's 2023 text explicitly includes legally owned or controlled data expected to yield benefits but not recognized as assets; item 7 addresses revenue from their sale. Nonrecognition therefore does not directly mean no value in use. [T-main][P-MOF]","That accounting text is not permission for arbitrary use; item 2 also concerns restrictions on rights. It does not imply that all unrecognized data have value, much less that possession or entry in the accounts permits provision to any destination. This explains the boundaries of a historical text; actual rights, privacy, and professional judgments need their respective grounds. [P-MOF]","Trace tasks, independent skills, occupational opportunities, and meaning separately during technological transitions. Tool proficiency or occupational exposure cannot replace long-term outcomes. Before removing old support, check whether substitutes are usable and what interruption would cause. Protective stopping in immediate danger follows the relevant procedures; an ideal substitute cannot be made a reason to insist that danger continue. [T-main]","Connect to Parts X–XI, §§13.8–13.11, §§15.3.3–15.3.5, and §§16.23–16.24 for relationships, knowledge, feedback, and practice. The eight entry points are: 28.01, specific mechanisms of technological determination and social shaping; 28.02, layers of infrastructure, standards, and maintenance; 28.03, technological politics in Winner and STS; 28.04, platform governance and digital labor; 28.05, AI generation and levels of evidence; 28.06, responsibility chains in agents and automation; 28.07, data privacy and digital rights; and 28.08, technological substitution and loss of capability. Navigation does not mean all related original works have been read. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Identify the product version, binary, configuration, and complete deployment conditions.","Test interoperability, semantics, reliability, and actual usability layer by layer.","Specify log evidence for recommendations, requests, execution, states, and consequences separately.","Test whether a refusal signal can reach the executor before the irreversible point.","Check distinct data permissions and deletion endpoints; assess substitution, interruptions, skills, and opportunities."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Redundancy in number","leftText":"Multiple units of equipment.","rightTitle":"Independent redundancy","rightText":"Requires checking shared upstream failures."},{"leftTitle":"Tool request","leftText":"A request to perform an action.","rightTitle":"State change","rightText":"Requires evidence of actual completion; consequences still need separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Accounting recognition","leftText":"Treatment under specified financial standards.","rightTitle":"Permission to use","rightText":"Rights and restrictions on use require separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"Correction of a technical quantity","leftText":"Reanalysis of an indicator.","rightTitle":"Improvement for users","rightText":"Requires evidence of real downstream outcomes."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: by the time reject is clicked, the backend action is irreversible, so approval offers no real veto.","Source boundary: two devices share an upstream power supply; quantity does not guarantee independent redundancy.","Teaching counterexample: retrieving a real publication still does not establish that it supports the present sentence."],"questions":["Does a bibliographic item's existence prove the sentence?","Are two devices necessarily ten times as reliable?","Does unrecognized data mean valueless data?","Is a reject button sufficient for oversight?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/figures/d28-deploy.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The SEC's 2013 administrative order on Knight's deployment controls","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The SEC's 2013 administrative order on Knight's deployment controls","text":"Paragraphs 15–22 describe one of eight servers remaining unupdated in 2012, absence of review by a second person, and 97 emails not designed as alerts.","sourceLabel":"SEC: In the Matter of Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16; §II and §III, paragraphs 15–22","boundary":"The findings' status within a settled order is explicit; this is neither a criminal conviction nor a single-cause explanation of all losses.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.28","start_line":5146,"end_line":5161,"startLine":5146,"endLine":5161}],"outputId":"section-d-28","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: shared power and independent redundancy","text":"In an artificial model, one device's failure probability is 0.1. If two devices are fully independent and either can provide service, total failure has probability 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.01. If both depend only on the same power supply, whose failure probability is 0.1, and everything else always works, total failure remains 0.1. Equal device counts do not guarantee equal reliability.","assumptions":"Two deliberately separate simplified models; upstream and internal faults are not mixed.","boundary":"These are not safety probabilities or design certification for a real system."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a button without a veto","text":"An artificial task enters an irreversible external stage at second 2, shows approval only at second 3, and receives rejection at second 4. Even if rejection is logged, it is later than the critical threshold. Only if rejection instead arrives at second 1 and actually blocks execution does this task have a prior veto path.","assumptions":"A fictional irreversible threshold and precise timing.","boundary":"Not every system can be reversed this simply, and the example confers no authority to execute."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: by the time reject is clicked, the backend action is irreversible, so approval offers no real veto.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: two devices share an upstream power supply; quantity does not guarantee independent redundancy.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: retrieving a real publication still does not establish that it supports the present sentence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a bibliographic item's existence prove the sentence?","answer":"No. Read whether the original supports the claim, then check the facts and eligibility for the intended use.","basis":"The evidential layer."},{"question":"Are two devices necessarily ten times as reliable?","answer":"No. Shared upstream dependencies can cause simultaneous failure.","basis":"Failure structure."},{"question":"Does unrecognized data mean valueless data?","answer":"No. The specified accounting text distinguishes expected benefits from recognition conditions.","basis":"Scope."},{"question":"Is a reject button sufficient for oversight?","answer":"No. Time before action, capacity, evidence, and a real blocking path are required.","basis":"The execution chain."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/figures/d28-deploy.png","alt":"Check physical properties, protocols, configuration, institutions, maintenance, and use together; it is not half technology and half society.","caption":"Based on D.28."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/figures/d28-actions.png","alt":"Five stages: recommendation, request, execution, external state, and consequence. Rejection must take effect beforehand.","caption":"Illustrative timing."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/figures/d28-redundancy.png","alt":"Two independent failures: 0.01; total failure through shared power: 0.1.","caption":"Teaching construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures distinguish deployment conditions, the five stages, and a shared-failure calculation.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["The reliability of no particular AI service or real deployment was evaluated.","The accounting text is not used to judge a firm's current eligibility to use data or transfer it across borders."],"taskId":"S-D-28","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.28, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4967–4982","claim_support":["Technological deployment and standards, AI evidence and the five stages, data permissions, substitution, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC: In the Matter of Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16; §II and §III, paragraphs 15–22","claim_support":["The 2012 deployment left one of eight servers unupdated and lacked a second-person review. The 97 emails were not designed as alerts and were not acted on before the market opened."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified paragraphs of the original PDF and the order's status. These are findings recorded in a settled administrative order; apart from exceptions such as jurisdiction, the respondent neither admitted nor denied them. They are not rewritten as a criminal conviction."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-MOF","title":"Ministry of Finance: Interim Provisions on Accounting Treatment Related to Enterprise Data Resources","url":"https://www.mof.gov.cn/jrttts/202308/P020230822648189970789.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Text released in 2023; the supplementary provisions specify entry into force on 2024-01-01","locator":"2023 attachment, printed pages 1–4; Part I and items 2, 3, and 7 of Part II","claim_support":["The scope includes legally owned or controlled data expected to generate economic benefits but not meeting asset-recognition conditions. Item 7 addresses revenue from selling data not recognized as assets; item 2 concerns restrictions on rights."],"limits":["The lesson author directly read the specified PDF sections. This explains only the scope of that text, not current operational accounting advice for enterprises. Accounting treatment is not permission for arbitrary use or trade."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.28　技术、基础设施、平台与人工智能; Chinese source lines 5420–5435; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/source/S-D-28.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-28/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"09e6f12ad13a7265c69eb38d9a0f97730a50809427316d8580b6a87de7efbaf7","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-28.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-28.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-28.md"};function d(u,e,p,h,m,g){const t=a("SectionLesson");return i(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-28" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.28</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Technology, Infrastructure, Platforms, and Artificial Intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-28" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.28 Technology, Infrastructure, Platforms, and Artificial Intelligence {#section-d-28}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Technical outcomes arise from specific combinations of physical properties, configuration, institutions, and maintenance. Nominal approval, tool requests, and changes in real-world states cannot be conflated.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §10–11, §13.8–13.11, §15.3.3–15.3.5, and §16.23–16.24. Research entries cover 28.01–28.08.</p><p>The material properties of technology, protocols, deployment configurations, institutions, maintenance, and actual use jointly determine specific outcomes. This does not mean that technology and society each account for half. Bandwidth, materials, and interfaces can each impose constraints in their own right. Default configurations and commercial arrangements can also change who is reached. Evaluate compliance with written standards, interoperability between implementations, consistency of business semantics, reliability under stress, and whether people can actually use the technology at their respective levels.</p><p>Maintenance depends on spare parts whose quality can be verified, skills, budgets, permissions, and ongoing labor. Several sets of equipment may share one upstream power supply or the same defective software. Numbers alone do not guarantee independent redundancy. Platform matching and flexibility in working hours can bring real benefits. Pricing, eligibility, future opportunities, and appeals can also be centrally controlled. The presence of an algorithm cannot by itself determine all aspects of legal employment status.</p><p>For AI, distinguish the attribution of generated content, support in the source text, external facts, and eligibility for the intended use. Retrieval helps obtain material and provides no guarantee of its truth. Internal consistency can help detect one class of errors and can also reproduce errors reliably. Suggestions, tool requests, execution of a procedure, changes to external state, and consequences are five distinct stages. Human oversight requires access to the relevant material, capability, time to review beforehand, and a manageable workload. A rejection signal must actually reach the actuator.</p><p>Teaching example. A user clicks Reject after the task in the backend has become irrevocable. Approval in name only does not provide a genuine veto. For privacy, distinguish collection, inference, reuse, retention, and downstream deletion. Issuing a deletion instruction does not establish that deletion has been completed. Assess actual possession of data, permitted use, recognition for accounting purposes, transactions, and eligibility to supply the data across borders separately. A lack of recognition as an asset for accounting purposes does not imply a lack of use or value (see Ministry of Finance, 2023). It also does not lift applicable restrictions on use. When studying technological transitions, track tasks, independent skills, career opportunities, and meaning separately. Tool proficiency and occupational exposure do not stand in for long-term outcomes. Before removing an older support that still serves a necessary function, check the availability of a substitute and the consequences of disruption. Stopping activity and taking protective measures in the face of immediate danger are handled separately under the applicable procedures.</p><p>Comparisons of technological necessity must specify what is held fixed. This could be the product name, the binary, the configuration, or the complete deployment including training, permissions, and maintenance. If the later items in that list change, the comparison can no longer be described as a change in institutions alone. The search for alternatives must also hold the task, resources, time limit, and necessary protections constant. Assess attribution of AI-generated content, the existence of bibliographic entries, metadata, source-text support, facts about the world, and permission for the use in question individually. A limited reanalysis or a recalculation using synthetic data can correct a technical quantity. It cannot be represented as evidence that actual users have experienced the corresponding improvement.</p><p>Research topics. 28.01 Technological determinism and the social shaping of technology. 28.02 Infrastructure, standards, and maintenance. 28.03 The politics of technology and sociotechnical systems (Winner, STS, and others). 28.04 Platform governance, algorithmic management, and digital labor. 28.05 AI knowledge, generation, and evidence. 28.06 AI agents, automation, and responsibility. 28.07 Data, privacy, surveillance, and digital rights. 28.08 Technological transitions, substitution, and loss of capabilities.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.28"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=n(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
